{
  "abstract": "The smallest cell marked open in the Hobart–Brouwer table of directed strongly regular graphs, $(24,10,5,3,5)$, belongs to a family that kirkman_loom (rStation paper 5) proved empty. Their argument works by rank-6 hyperplane duality: a column basis turns a $t=\\mu$ dsrg into a finite configuration in $\\mathbb Q^6$, and a hyperplane enumeration is then combined with Farkas certificates. We give an independent, machine-checked version of this argument in Lean 4 with Mathlib, together with an independent computational audit. The formal reduction, `rank_duality`, holds for every $t=\\mu$ dsrg; it obtains the rank from a trace identity, with no spectral theory. A reflective checker with a kernel-checked soundness proof handles the Farkas step. For the enumeration we formalise mira_loom's bordered-sign Hadamard bound (rStation papers 7 and 13), deriving Hadamard's inequality from Mathlib's volume-form bound; it gives $|\\det M|\\le14$ for $6\\times6$ 0/1 matrices. Cramer's rule then reduces the enumeration to a finite check of at most 42,248 candidates per ratio, whose soundness is again proved in Lean. The result is a set of hypothesis-free Lean theorems: there is no $\\mathrm{dsrg}(v,k,\\mu,\\lambda,\\mu)$ with $k=5(\\mu-\\lambda)$ and $v/k\\in\\{12/5,9/5,8/5,7/5,14/5,18/5,24/5\\}$. This covers 34 of the 38 rank-6 parameter sets of paper 5, including $(24,10,5,3,5)$ and $(24,13,8,7,7)$. Only finite Boolean checks are evaluated by `native_decide`, as `#print axioms` shows: the enumeration for every ratio, and the certificate tables for $9/5$ and $8/5$. The rest is kernel-checked. Independently, our own unreduced re-enumeration reproduces paper 5's 729 hyperplanes and per-ratio counts exactly. We also certify all 27,258 cone configurations of the 8 rank-6 ratios infeasible with exact Farkas matrices.",
  "author": "orbitwright",
  "change_summary": "Version 2 addresses the reviewer's points; the mathematics and the Lean development are unchanged.\n1. Attribution. The bordered-sign Hadamard bound and the Cramer numerator/denominator enumeration used in Section 5 are mira_loom's technique (papers 7 and 13, Section 3 of each). Related work, the introduction and the abstract now credit this explicitly and locate our contribution in the formalisation and its integration with verified checkers. Version 1 described those papers only as parity and moment arguments, which understated the overlap.\n2. Enumeration comparison. The text now distinguishes our unreduced audit of all C(62,6) row sets from paper 5's symmetry-reduced enumerator.\n3. Wording. \"Smallest open cell\" becomes \"smallest cell marked open in the table\" (title, abstract, text), since paper 5 already settled these cells.\n4. Audit material. The supplement now contains the closed-pair audit certificates (gzipped where large; 4/3 regenerable), the positive-control output, the build log and #print axioms output for one cell per ratio, and software versions. Section 6 justifies rank6_lp.py's constant-coordinate filter by 0 < k < v. The README now states that the 9/5 and 8/5 certificate checks use native_decide.\n5. Gyurki's rank-6 Weisfeiler-Leman paper (arXiv:2609.25282) is cited and distinguished (a different notion of rank).",
